   "is_accepted": true,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/2983185",
   "score": 11,
   "created_utc": "2010-06-06T05:10:57Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2010-06-06T05:10:57Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5",
   "body_html": "<p><em>new</em> and <em>delete</em> are C++ <strong>operators</strong> (like +, (), etc.) whereas <em>malloc</em> and <em>free</em> are (C) functions. Some operators (including new and delete) can be overloaded.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 3044764,
   "parent_id": 2983182,
   "parent_type": "question",
   "author_user_id": 55078,
   "author_display_name": "Juliano",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/2983182/#comment3044764_2983182",
   "score": 7,
   "created_utc": "2010-06-06T05:12:12Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5",
   "body_html": "New and delete are not &quot;preprocessors&quot;. They have nothing to do with <i>the</i> preprocessor. New and delete are language directives. More precisely, they are unary operators like &#39;+&#39; and &#39;*&#39;."
  }
